From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Mon, 1 Aug 94 12:42:56 GMT
Subject: [#94-8-1] fuzzy prisoner's dilemma?

 Anton Sherwood <dasher@netcom.com> writes: 
>
>Has there ever been a fuzzy version of Axelrod's Iterated Prisoner's
>Dilemma tournament?  By "fuzzy", I mean with choices and scores
>not restricted to defect (0) and cooperate (1), but including all
>real numbers between.  The scoring function could be 
>	a - bx + cy + dxy
>where x is your move, y is the other move, 0 <= x,y <= 1,
>0 < b < c, (b-c)/2 < d < b.  (For Axelrod's PD, a=b=1, c=4, d=-1.)
>
I've never heard of one, but it sounds like a really cool idea--
and just to make it a little more like the real world, I'd add
that the competitors win resources that they can
use for reward and punishment in future rounds--but there
are maintenance costs for keeping a large stash.

I'm not sure what the most elegant way is to handle the maintenance
costs. Just having a cost that goes up as a player accumulates
points (or that maximizes at some moderate level of points and
then declines per point as more points are added) is arbitrary,
though it would be fun to fiddle with the cost.

You could get some interesting results by allowing competitors to
transfer mainentance ability to each other. In effect, the big
winners could hire guards/accountants, and if there's a declining
cost per point for sufficiently large stashes, then it would be
worthwhile for the small players to form cooperatives. 

A PD variant that I've wondered about is allowing players to
interact with all the other players during a round, but limiting
the amount (or perhaps type) of memory that they have for previous
transactions. By type of memory, I mean that some players might
be better at remembering who they've played with and others
might be able to remember more extended histories.

This reminds me of a type of competition that I'd like to see--
a completely open chess tournament--open to humans, teams of
humans, programs, teams of programs, and human-program combinations.

I believe that this would lead to some good interface designs,
not to mention a chance to discover what the current upper limit
for chess playing is.

Nancy Lebovitz

