>Has there ever been a fuzzy version of Axelrod's Iterated Prisoner's
>Dilemma tournament?  By "fuzzy", I mean with choices and scores
>not restricted to defect (0) and cooperate (1), but including all
>real numbers between.  The scoring function could be 
>	a - bx + cy + dxy
>where x is your move, y is the other move, 0 <= x,y <= 1,
>0 < b < c, (b-c)/2 < d < b.  (For Axelrod's PD, a=b=1, c=4, d=-1.)

I've never heard of this, but it sounds quite reasonable and very interesting.
For one, you could apply more "analog" algorithms to search for solutions,
such as reinforcement learning or a GA with an analog coding.  Then small
variations in players could translate into small statistical advantages and
solutions could smoothly explore the smoothly changing fitness function.
Perhaps this problem formulation could afford a dynamical systems analysis...
(I assume that you mean an Iterated PD).

I've never heard of such a thing myself, but I'll keep an eye out...

Paul


